config SOC_ANDES_V5_PMA
	bool "Andes V5 Physical Memory Attribute (PMA)"
	default y
	select ARCH_HAS_NOCACHE_MEMORY_SUPPORT
	select HAS_HPMSDK_PMP
	help
		This option enables the Andes V5 PMA, in order to support SW to
		configure physical memory attribute by PMA CSRs. The address
		matching of Andes V5 PMA is like RISC-V PMP NAPOT mode
		(power-of-two alignment).

config HPM_SOC_CPU_FREQ_MHZ
	int "CPU0/CPU1 core frequency (MHz)"
	default 600
	range 24 600
	help
		Core clock for CPU0 and CPU1, reached with PLL0CLK0's
		post-divider. The VCO is left exactly as the BootROM preset
		set it.

		This is a CEILING, not an exact value. The post-divider steps
		in 0.2 increments -- index n divides by (1.0 + 0.2n) -- so from
		the preset's 600 MHz VCO the reachable set is 600 / 500 /
		428.6 / 375 / 333.3 / 300 MHz. soc_init_clock() picks the first
		step at or below this number, so 400 lands on 375 and 480 lands
		on 428.6. Rounding down is what keeps the VDD_SOC bin (selected
		from this number against datasheet table 12) valid by
		construction. Asking for less than about 41 MHz saturates the
		divider and lands above the request, which is harmless only
		because every voltage bin ceiling is far above that.

		Changing this moves the two cores and nothing else. Measured on
		hpm6e00evk with `hs2clk`: of the SYSCTL clock nodes, exactly
		two select a PLL0 output, and they are CLK_TOP_CPU0 and
		CLK_TOP_CPU1. AHB0, XPI0 (the XIP flash!) and the rest of the
		peripheral tree sit on PLL1CLK0, so the ADC convert-clock
		ceiling (RM 71.2.1.1) does not move either.

		Do NOT reach the target by retuning the VCO with
		pllctlv2_init_pll_with_freq() instead. That was tried and it
		broke UART0 outright -- see the comment in soc_init_clock().
		HPM's own board support never writes MFI/MFN on this part.

		Under sysbuild this reaches the DEFAULT image only. Passing
		-DCONFIG_HPM_SOC_CPU_FREQ_MHZ=400 leaves MCUboot on 600, and
		that asymmetry is the intended arrangement, not an oversight:

		  - MCUboot is the recovery net. A clock configuration that has
		    not been proven belongs in the image you can replace over
		    USB, not in the one whose failure mode is ROM ISP.
		  - It saves nothing. MCUboot runs for well under a second on a
		    normal boot.
		  - It costs something. ECDSA-P256 verification and the
		    swap-using-scratch move over a 1536K slot both scale with
		    the core clock, so a slower bootloader lengthens DFU and
		    factory programming -- watch station timeouts.

		The hazard is not the mismatch, it is an UNNOTICED mismatch:
		this option silently applying to one image and not the other
		is exactly how a broken low-frequency path first looked like
		two unrelated bugs. To move MCUboot deliberately, pass
		-Dmcuboot_CONFIG_HPM_SOC_CPU_FREQ_MHZ=... as well, and expect
		the two images to report different core clocks until you do.

		WARNING: the core1 8 kHz engine budget is 94 us (XInput) / 96 us
		(DS4) of a 125 us period at 600 MHz. Scaling is roughly linear
		for ITCM-resident work, so 480 MHz leaves single-digit
		microseconds of margin. Re-measure the hot path before shipping
		anything below 600.

config HPM_SOC_GATE_UNUSED_CLOCKS
	bool "Leave unused peripheral clocks gated at boot"
	default n
	help
		Gates the motor-control island (QEI/QEO/RDC/MTG/VSC/CLC/PLB/
		SEI/EMDS), PWM1-3 and PTPC, none of which has an enabled
		devicetree node on the boards this fork carries.

		DEFAULT IS n BECAUSE THIS BROKE THE STICKS. Measured on
		hpm6e00evk with the internal AFE: with the option on, the analog
		sticks read dead. The ADC trigger chain is PWM0 TRGO -> TRGM0 ->
		ADC STRGI (trig-base/trig-input-src/trig-num in the board
		overlays), and HPM_TRGM0_BASE 0xF047C000 sits inside the motor
		island's address block, between HPM_SEI_BASE 0xF0470000 and
		HPM_MTG0_BASE 0xF0490000. TRGM has NO sysctl_resource_* of its
		own, so it is clocked with its neighbours -- and removing them
		from group 0 takes the trigger mux with them.

		Read the selection rule that failed before re-enabling this.
		"Nothing in the devicetree uses it" is NOT a safe criterion:
		TRGM has no devicetree node and no clock binding at all. The
		ADC driver reaches it through the raw base address from
		trig-base and writes its registers directly, so nothing in the
		system ever declares that it needs TRGM's clock. Every check
		passed: four board configurations built clean, and `hs2clk`
		reported "0 unexpected" because TRGM has no resource id and is
		invisible to that readback.

		And it bought nothing measurable. Bench A/B on hpm6e00evk found
		no detectable change in supply current with the option on. The
		only lever that moved power on this part was the VDD_SOC bin,
		which CONFIG_HPM_SOC_CPU_FREQ_MHZ selects.

		If you do re-enable it: verify the analog sticks, not just boot
		and enumeration, and treat any peripheral reached by a raw base
		address as clocked-by-someone-else until proven otherwise.

		PWM0 is NOT covered by this option: it is the ADC TRGO source
		(trigger-pwm in the board overlays) and stays ungated always.

		Set n to restore the stock HPMicro behaviour, e.g. when bringing
		up a board that does use the motor peripherals.

endif # SOC_SERIES_HPM6E00